prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a 3D interactive simulation of optical prism refraction and dispersion, calculating light-ray paths based on Snell's law.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Prism Geometry]
(1) Triangular prism: an isosceles triangular cross-section (apex angle 60°), extruded from a triangular Shape using ExtrudeGeometry, with a depth of approximately 2 units;
(2) Material: MeshPhysicalMaterial, transmission=0.9, roughness=0, ior=1.5 (for visualization; actual refraction must be calculated using Snell's law); for the transparent effect, set transparent=true, opacity=0.3;
(3) Place the prism at the center of the scene, with its triangular cross-section in the XY plane and one face (the incident face) facing left;

[Incident White Light]
(4) A beam of white light enters from the left: use a thin white cylinder (CylinderGeometry, radius=0.02, aligned with the light-ray direction) or Line3 + LineGeometry;
(5) The light ray starts outside the scene on the left (x=-10), and its direction is controlled by lightAngle (the angle relative to the normal of the prism's incident face);

[Snell's Law Refraction Calculation]
(6) At the intersection of the light ray and the prism's first face, apply Snell's law: n1 * sin(θ1) = n2 * sin(θ2);
(7) n1 = 1.0 (air), n2 = dispersive refractive index (different for each color);
(8) Refractive indices for 7 colors:
  - Red:    n=1.510, color=#FF0000
  - Orange: n=1.512, color=#FF7F00
  - Yellow: n=1.514, color=#FFFF00
  - Green:  n=1.517, color=#00FF00
  - Blue:   n=1.521, color=#0000FF
  - Indigo: n=1.524, color=#4B0082
  - Violet: n=1.528, color=#8B00FF
(9) The light rays propagate inside the prism to the second face, where Snell's law is applied again (n2→n1=1.0) as they exit the prism;
(10) Calculate the normal direction of the second face (based on the orientation of the triangle's second face), and apply the reverse Snell's law;
(11) The 7 exiting light rays must each have a different deflection angle, forming a dispersed spectrum;

[Exiting Light-Ray Visualization]
(12) Draw each of the 7 exiting light rays as a line in its corresponding color (LineBasicMaterial), extending toward the right side of the scene for a length of at least 10 units;
(13) Place a white projection screen (PlaneGeometry, positioned vertically) to the right of the prism, where the 7 light rays strike the screen to form a rainbow band;

[Control Panel]
(14) Prism rotation slider id="prismAngle", range 0-60, step 1, default 0; rotate the prism around the Z axis (changing the direction of the incident face);
(15) Base refractive index slider id="refractiveIndex", range 1.3-1.8, step 0.01, default 1.52; scale the refractive indices of all colors proportionally (preserving the relative proportions of the dispersion differences);
(16) Light angle slider id="lightAngle", range 20-70, step 1, default 45; controls the incident-light angle (in degrees);
(17) Normal display checkbox id="showNormals" (checkbox): when checked, display normal vectors on both refracting faces (short yellow arrows);

[Mathematical and Physical Accuracy]
(18) All angle calculations must correctly apply Snell's law: at each interface, calculate the angle of incidence (the angle between the light-ray direction and the face normal), then use sin(θ_refracted) = (n1/n2) * sin(θ_incident);
(19) Handle total internal reflection: if sin(θ_refracted) > 1, the light ray of that color undergoes total internal reflection (represent it with a dashed line or do not draw the exiting ray);
(20) The deviation angle of each exiting light ray = angle of incidence - angle of refraction (incident face) + angle of incidence - angle of refraction (exit face) + prism apex angle, and must be calculated correctly;

[State Exposure (Required)]
(21) Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame, containing:
  - prismAngle: number (prism rotation angle in degrees)
  - refractiveIndex: number (base refractive index)
  - lightAngle: number (incident-light angle in degrees)
  - showNormals: boolean
  - outputAngles: number[7] (deviation angles of the 7 exiting light rays, in degrees, ordered from red to violet)
  - totalDeviation: number (total deviation angle in degrees for the middle color (Green))

Output a single HTML file that can run when opened directly in a browser.
